    <!-- Question 1 -->
    <div class="accordion-item">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span>Q1: What is the Node.js Event Loop, and how does it achieve high concurrency despite being single-threaded?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▼</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <p>
          Node.js executes JavaScript on a single main thread via Google's V8 engine. High concurrency is achieved through <strong>asynchronous non-blocking I/O</strong> mediated by <strong>Libuv</strong>. When an asynchronous operation (such as a database query, file read, or network request) is initiated, Node offloads the heavy lifting to the operating system kernel or the Libuv worker thread pool. The main thread continues processing subsequent requests without waiting. When the I/O finishes, its callback is enqueued onto the Callback Queue, and the Event Loop transfers it to the Call Stack as soon as the stack becomes clear.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Question 2 -->
    <div class="accordion-item">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span>Q2: What is the difference between <code>req.params</code>, <code>req.query</code>, and <code>req.body</code> in Express.js?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▼</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <ul>
          <li><strong><code>req.params</code>:</strong> Contains route parameters defined in the path with a colon prefix (e.g., in <code>/api/products/:id</code>, accessing <code>/api/products/42</code> produces <code>{ id: '42' }</code>).</li>
          <li><strong><code>req.query</code>:</strong> Contains URL query string parameters appended after a <code>?</code> (e.g., in <code>/api/products?category=Audio&amp;sort=asc</code>, produces <code>{ category: 'Audio', sort: 'asc' }</code>).</li>
          <li><strong><code>req.body</code>:</strong> Contains payload data submitted in the HTTP request body (e.g., via JSON or HTML form submission). Requires body-parsing middleware such as <code>express.json()</code> or <code>express.urlencoded()</code> to populate.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Question 3 -->
    <div class="accordion-item">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span>Q3: What is Express middleware, and what happens if you forget to invoke <code>next()</code>?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▼</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <p>
          Middleware functions are functions that have access to the request object (<code>req</code>), response object (<code>res</code>), and the <code>next</code> function in the application's request-response cycle. If a middleware function does not end the request-response cycle (e.g., by calling <code>res.send()</code>, <code>res.json()</code>, or <code>res.render()</code>), it <strong>must</strong> invoke <code>next()</code> to pass control to the subsequent middleware function in the stack. If you forget to invoke <code>next()</code>, the HTTP request will hang indefinitely until the client times out.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Question 4 -->
    <div class="accordion-item">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span>Q4: Why should <code>&lt;%= %&gt;</code> be used instead of <code>&lt;%- %&gt;</code> when outputting user data in EJS?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▼</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <p>
          <code>&lt;%= %&gt;</code> automatically HTML-escapes special characters such as <code>&amp;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&quot;</code>, and <code>&#39;</code> into HTML entities (e.g. <code>&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;</code>). This prevents <strong>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</strong> attacks where an attacker injects malicious client-side JavaScript into database records. <code>&lt;%- %&gt;</code> renders raw unescaped HTML, which should strictly be reserved for trusted server templates (e.g., <code>&lt;%- include(...) %&gt;</code>).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Question 5 -->
    <div class="accordion-item">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span>Q5: How does an Express error-handling middleware function differ in signature from regular middleware?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▼</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <p>
          Error-handling middleware functions are explicitly identified by having <strong>four arguments</strong> instead of three: <code>(err, req, res, next)</code>. Even if you do not use the <code>next</code> object, Express inspects function arity (<code>fn.length === 4</code>) to differentiate error handlers from normal middleware. If an error is passed to <code>next(err)</code>, Express skips all subsequent regular middleware and routes directly to the registered 4-parameter error handler.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Question 6 -->
    <div class="accordion-item">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span>Q6: What is the purpose of Nodemon, and how does it detect changes?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▼</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <p>
          In standard Node.js, server code changes require manually killing the node process and restarting it. <strong>Nodemon</strong> is a CLI development utility that wraps around your Node application, monitors the directory tree for filesystem modification events on specified extensions (<code>.js</code>, <code>.json</code>, <code>.ejs</code>), and automatically restarts the Node server process.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Question 7 -->
    <div class="accordion-item">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span>Q7: What are the primary HTTP status code families, and when should they be returned in a REST API?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▼</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>200 OK:</strong> Standard success for GET, PUT, and DELETE operations.</li>
          <li><strong>201 Created:</strong> Resource successfully created via POST.</li>
          <li><strong>400 Bad Request:</strong> Client sent invalid payload or failed input validation.</li>
          <li><strong>404 Not Found:</strong> The requested route or resource ID does not exist.</li>
          <li><strong>500 Internal Server Error:</strong> Unhandled runtime exception occurred on the server.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Question 8 -->
    <div class="accordion-item">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span>Q8: What is the difference between <code>res.send()</code>, <code>res.json()</code>, and <code>res.render()</code>?</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▼</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <ul>
          <li><strong><code>res.send()</code>:</strong> Sends generic HTTP response body (string, Buffer, or object) and auto-sets the <code>Content-Type</code>.</li>
          <li><strong><code>res.json()</code>:</strong> Explicitly serializes an object into a JSON string and sets the <code>Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8</code> header.</li>
          <li><strong><code>res.render()</code>:</strong> Invokes the configured template engine (EJS), compiles the specified view with passed variables, and sends the resulting HTML string with <code>Content-Type: text/html</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

